# Principality of prod 𝔪_{P_i}^{mᵢ} versus sum mᵢ Pᵢ = O

Theorem statement: `WeierstrassCurve.Affine.CoordinateRing.isPrincipal_prod_XYIdeal_zpow_iff`
(`4786f617-f117-588c-bf77-9a1284619e9e`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `3ad29414-b8b3-5ea6-b1c8-eee65a2dade7`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $F$ be a field, $W$ a Weierstrass curve over $F$, and let $\iota$ be a type with a finite subset
$s \subseteq \iota$. Given families $x, y : \iota \to F$ and a hypothesis $h$ asserting that for
every $i$ the pair $(x_i, y_i)$ is a nonsingular point of the affine curve attached to $W$, together
with a family of integers $m : \iota \to \mathbb{Z}$, form the element $\prod_{i \in s}
\mathtt{XYIdeal'}(h_i)^{m_i}$ of the unit group of the fractional ideals of the coordinate ring
$F[W]$ inside the function field $F(W)$, where $\mathtt{XYIdeal'}(h_i)$ is the invertible fractional
ideal attached to the point $(x_i, y_i)$, namely the ideal generated by $X - x_i$ and $Y - y_i$; the
powers are integer powers taken in that unit group. The assertion is that the underlying
$F[W]$-submodule of $F(W)$ of this product is principal if and only if $\sum_{i \in s} m_i \cdot
\mathtt{Point.some}\,(x_i)\,(y_i)\,(h_i) = 0$, the sum being taken in the group of points of $W$
with its group law, with $\mathbb{Z}$-scalar multiplication. No injectivity is required of $i
\mapsto (x_i, y_i)$, and the $m_i$ are arbitrary integers.

This is the elliptic-curve case of the Abel–Jacobi criterion for principal divisors: a divisor
supported on affine points is the affine part of a principal divisor exactly when the corresponding
sum of points vanishes in the group law. It is used in the construction of the functions whose
divisors are supported on torsion points, and is cited here in the computation of the valuation of
the Weil numerator,
[`WeierstrassCurve.Affine.valuation_weilNum`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.Affine.valuation_weilNum).

